                <h2>Key Takeaway: Top-up ≠ Spend ≠ Invoice—These Numbers Will Never Match by Default</h2>
<p>If you've ever run overseas advertising campaigns, you've likely faced this frustration: you topped up $100,000, the platform shows $98,000 in spend, but the invoice reads $95,000. The finance team is confused, and the media buyer can't explain the discrepancy.</p>
<p><strong>The core issue is that top-up, spend, and invoices come from three different system nodes, each with its own calculation logic and settlement cycle.</strong> Top-up is a fund deposit action, spend is real-time ad consumption, and invoice is the platform's periodic billing statement. Without clarifying the relationship between these three figures, finance will never reconcile.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Typical Scenarios That Haunt Finance Teams</h2>
<h3>Scenario 1: Prepaid Balance "Disappears"</h3>
<p>Major platforms like Meta, Google, and TikTok operate on a prepaid model—funds are deposited first, then deducted as ads run. The problem: there's a time lag between fund deposit, spend deduction, and invoice generation.</p>
<p>Take a tool app's monthly statement as an example:</p>
<ul>
<li>Top-up: $30,000 on the 10th (credited same day)</li>
<li>Cumulative spend from 10th–25th: $28,500</li>
<li>Platform service fee auto-deducted at month-end: $800</li>
<li>Actual available balance: $30,000 - $28,500 - $800 = $700 (but the system may show a different balance due to delayed updates)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Operational insight</strong>: Don't just check the dashboard balance after topping up—record each top-up's timestamp and amount as your reconciliation starting point.</p>
<h3>Scenario 2: Exchange Rate Gaps Quietly Eat Into Profit</h3>
<p>For multi-currency settlements, top-up rates, spend rates, and invoice rates often differ. Consider a cross-border e-commerce team topping up a USD account with CNY:</p>
<ul>
<li>Top-up rate: 7.25 (bank's real-time rate)</li>
<li>Spend rate: 7.20 (platform's internal settlement rate)</li>
<li>Invoice rate: 7.18 (platform's monthly settlement rate)</li>
</ul>
<p>For a $100,000 top-up, the exchange rate variance is approximately $700. If finance books based on the top-up amount, profit calculations will be distorted.</p>
<p><strong>Operational insight</strong>: Maintain a currency variance ledger, recording the rate for each top-up, spend, and invoice, then reconcile differences monthly.</p>
<h3>Scenario 3: Platform Rebates Not Factored Into Costs</h3>
<p>Both Google and Meta offer tiered rebate policies—for example, 5%-10% rebate if quarterly spend reaches certain thresholds. However, rebates are typically returned as <strong>ad Credits</strong>, not itemized on invoices.</p>
<p>One gaming team reported: $500,000 quarterly spend with an 8% rebate agreement ($40,000). But finance reconciliation missed this credit, making the report show a loss when the campaign was actually profitable.</p>
<p><strong>Operational insight</strong>: Verify rebate credits each quarter and offset them against advertising costs in your profit reports.</p>
<hr />
<h2>A Practical Reconciliation SOP</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Build a Top-up Ledger</h3>
<p>For each top-up, record:</p>
<ul>
<li>Top-up date, amount, currency</li>
<li>Top-up rate (bank or platform rate)</li>
<li>Corresponding account ID</li>
<li>Top-up channel (Alipay/bank card/platform direct top-up)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Pull Daily Spend Reports</h3>
<p>Use automation tools (e.g., DatawPortal or platform APIs) to pull spend data daily and compare against your top-up ledger. If spend spikes unexpectedly, it could indicate invalid traffic or mis-targeting—act quickly to minimize loss.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Verify Monthly Invoices</h3>
<p>Platform invoices are typically issued on the 5th–10th of the following month. Key verification points:</p>
<ul>
<li>Does invoice amount match dashboard spend?</li>
<li>If discrepancies exist, document reasons (refunds, ad Credit offsets, etc.)</li>
<li>Keep both original and digital copies as expense documentation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 4: Quarterly Rebate Audit</h3>
<p>At each quarter-end, confirm rebate credits with your platform account manager. Convert Credit amounts to actual cost offsets and update profit reports.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Financial Reconciliation Is the First Line of Defense in Ad Risk Management</h2>
<p>Many teams focus only on ROI and spend volume, overlooking the importance of financial reconciliation. However, <strong>the consistency of top-up, spend, and invoices directly reflects your control over ad accounts.</strong> Persistent discrepancies often signal unnoticed risks—abnormal spending, platform settlement bugs, or internal financial process gaps.</p>
<p>If your team struggles with ad reconciliation, feel free to contact us. Yiyu Digital Media offers one-stop services including multi-account unified top-up, spend monitoring, and invoice management, helping cross-border teams achieve closed-loop financial data control.</p>
